  "final_message": "I wrote `answer.json` with three functions. I didn't change any code, and I didn't run the tests.\n\n- **`usage.report_usage`**: it takes the client's millisecond timestamp and stores it as-is. It would have to convert that to seconds, and its fallback default currently uses `to_millis(ctx.clock.now())`.\n- **`usage.records_in_period`**: it converts the period bounds to milliseconds to compare against stored timestamps. It would have to compare in seconds instead.\n- **`api.report_usage`**: it echoes `record[\"timestamp\"]` back in the response. That value would now be in seconds, so it would have to convert back to milliseconds to keep the public API unchanged.\n\nThe other places that touch timestamps don't need changes:\n- `engine.report_usage` only passes the value through.\n- `usage.aggregate_usage` and `usage.usage_summary` go through `records_in_period`.\n- `store.usage_for` doesn't read the timestamp.\n- The `UsageRecord` type in `models` isn't a function.",
